==Trivia==
* Defeating Red at this location causes the [[Game credits|credits]] to roll. In [[Generation II]], progress is not [[save]]d and the player returns to the [[Pokémon Center]] outside Mt. Silver. In {{game|HeartGold and SoulSilver|s}}, progress is saved and the player returns to their home in [[New Bark Town]].
* Like [[Snowpoint City]], Mt. Silver's summit features {{DL|Easter egg|diamond dust}} on certain special days in {{game|HeartGold and SoulSilver|s}}, including the anniversary of the release of {{game|Gold and Silver|s}} in Japan.
** Mt. Silver is also the only location in Pokémon HeartGold and SoulSilver where a naturally occurring {{weather|hail}}storm can be experienced.
* In {{game|HeartGold and SoulSilver|s}}, whenever the player is in or on the mountain, the location header pop-up is always "Mt. Silver Cave", even when the player is not inside the cave. This may possibly be a carryover from the original games, in which the in-game location header was "Silver Cave".
* [[Fresh Water]] is collected from Mt. Silver, according to the Japanese item description in {{game2|Gold|Silver|Crystal}}, and {{g|Stadium 2}}. This is also mentioned in the Korean item description in Pokémon Gold and Silver. However, this is not mentioned in the English item descriptions.

===Origin===
Mt. Silver is based on {{wp|Mt. Fuji}} in the real-world {{wp|Japan}}.

